    "question": {
      ":question/id": "be86ea99-44c6-54c2-a612-fba95524278e",
      ":question/problem": "Given $y'(t)=t^{5}+6$, find $y(t)$, the antiderivative of $y'(t)$.\n\n$y(t)=$\n{{field-1}}\n$+\\,C$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "We know that $y'(t)=t^{5}+6$.\n\nApplying the sum rule, we compute the sum of the integrals of each function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\left(t^{5}+6\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t &= \\int \\left(t^{5}+6\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\ &= \\int t^{5}\\,\\mathrm{d}t+6\\int 1\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\ &= \\frac{t^{5+1}}{(5+1)}+6\\frac{t^{0+1}}{(0+1)}+C \\\\ &= \\frac{t^{6}}{6}+6t+C\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
