    "question": {
      ":question/id": "0ed01a1c-fc91-5bf6-b168-e05a25c14e8b",
      ":question/problem": "Given $y'(t)=-4-t^{6}$, find $y(t)$, the antiderivative of $y'(t)$.\n\n$y(t)=$\n{{field-1}}\n$+\\,C$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "We know that $y'(t)=-4-t^{6}$.\n\nApplying the sum rule, we compute the sum of the integrals of each function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\left(-4-t^{6}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t &= \\int \\left(-4-t^{6}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\ &= -4\\int 1\\,\\mathrm{d}t-\\int t^{6}\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\ &= -4\\frac{t^{0+1}}{(0+1)}-\\frac{t^{6+1}}{(6+1)}+C \\\\ &= -4t-\\frac{t^{7}}{7}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
